<DocsLayout
  title="Setups"
  subtitle="One setup, many operations, one emit per program. WP14 is in progress."
  description="Multi-operation CAM setups: plan, tool table, generate, and what is still missing on disk."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP14 on the task board is <strong>in progress</strong>. The board text says
      <code>cam.program</code> contracts, plan/generate, the post tool-number override, and
      multi-tool parsing are done, and that the desktop setup store and persistence are pending.
      Spec: <code>specs/14-multi-op-setup.md</code>. Owner directories named by that spec:
      <code>packages/core/src/cam/program</code>, post and analysis, 
      <code>packages/api/src/groups/cam-setup.ts</code>,
      <code>apps/sidecar/src/http/cam-setup.ts</code>, and
      <code>apps/desktop/src/features/cam/&#123;lib,store&#125;/setup*</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="The planner directory is not in the tree">
      <p>
        <code>packages/core/src/cam/program/</code> is absent.
        <code>packages/api/src/groups/cam-setup.ts</code> is absent.
        <code>apps/sidecar/src/http/cam-setup.ts</code> is absent. Spec 14 §5 says those program
        modules are built and that the desktop store and database persistence follow in later
        steps. Trust the files you can open. The contracts below are the spec you implement
        against. They are not a claim that <code>planPrograms</code> is importable today.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The model</h2>
    <p>
      The v6 generator takes one operation per request
      (<code>POST /api/toolpaths/generate</code>). The desktop multi-toolpath panel stitches texts
      with <code>combineGCode</code> in
      <code>apps/desktop/src/features/cam/lib/multiToolpath.ts</code>. Concatenating full programs
      leaves each block with its own header, <code>G21</code>, <code>M30</code>, and
      <code>%</code>. No tool change is emitted. A machine cannot run that as one job.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="Spec 14 §2">
{`Setup      = stock + machine + post + grouping + ordered Operations
Operation  = element ids + parameters + one Tool
ToolTable  = pocket T1..Tn -> Tool, derived from the operations
Program    = one emitted .nc; tool changes between operations

Operations -> planPrograms -> PlannedProgram[] -> generateSetup -> GeneratedProgram[]`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      Grouping is <code>single</code> (one program, tool changes inside it), <code>perTool</code>,
      or <code>perOperation</code>. All three come from the same planner.
      <code>planPrograms</code> is pure: no geometry and no clock.
      <code>generateSetup</code> calls the existing generator per operation, then emits each
      program once. There is no string concatenation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="disk" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What is on disk</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Spec names</th><th>In the tree</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>cam/program/op.ts</code>, <code>setup.ts</code>, <code>tooltable.ts</code>, <code>plan.ts</code>, <code>programs.ts</code></td><td>Directory missing. <code>cam/index.ts</code> does not export a <code>program</code> namespace.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PostOptions.toolNumbers</code>, <code>retractBeforeToolChange</code></td><td>Present in <code>packages/core/src/cam/post/types.ts</code>. <code>post/emitter.ts</code> applies both. Defaults keep prior output byte-identical.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>analyseGcode</code> splitting a multi-tool file</td><td><code>packages/core/src/cam/generator/analysis.ts</code> exports <code>analyseGcode</code>. Spec 14 says a tool change becomes its own toolpath so the viewport and verifier keep the tools.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>kernel/execute.ts</code> tool-number map</td><td>Present. It passes <code>toolNumbers</code> into <code>post.emit</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API group <code>camSetup</code></td><td>Group file not present. <code>packages/api/src/cam-program.ts</code> is the portable saved-program schema, a different contract.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sidecar <code>http/cam-setup.ts</code></td><td>Not present. Toolpath handlers live in <code>apps/sidecar/src/http/toolpaths.ts</code> and <code>toolpath-generator.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desktop <code>setupStore</code></td><td>Spec 14 §5 says this follows later. Do not document a store that is not in <code>features/cam</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cam/setup.ts</code></td><td>Present, and it is a different type: <code>ManufacturingSetup</code> (work frame, stock box, fixtures, tool assemblies) from spec 04, not the WP14 operation list.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="contracts" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Contracts</h2>
    <p>
      When the program module lands, these are the types spec 14 §3 requires. Geometry resolution
      stays outside core. <code>generateSetup(setup, geometryOf)</code> receives a function from
      operation to <code>GeometryInput</code> because core has no I/O.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>Operation</code></td><td><code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>elementIds</code>, <code>params</code>, <code>tool</code>, optional <code>pocket</code>, <code>enabled</code>, <code>fromPreviousStock</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Setup</code></td><td>Stock, machine type, work offset, <code>PostConfig</code>, grouping, <code>retractBeforeToolChange</code>, ordered operations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>PostConfig</code></td><td><code>postId</code> of <code>generic</code>, <code>fanuc</code>, or <code>grbl</code>, decimals, canned cycles, arc mode, header, tolerance, optional line numbers, stamp, <code>programNumberBase</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>buildToolTable</code></td><td>Pocket to tool, plus warnings. <code>assignPockets</code> keeps an explicit pocket, otherwise first use.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>planPrograms</code></td><td><code>SetupPlan</code>: programs, tool table, tool loads, tool changes, warnings. Warns when a tool appears in non-adjacent operations. <code>reorderByTool</code> is the UI fix.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generateSetup</code></td><td><code>GeneratedSetup</code>: per-program G-code and IR, totals for cut length, rapid length, and estimated seconds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>validateSetup</code></td><td>Issues, not throws: duplicate pocket, operation without elements, rpm above <code>tool.maxRpm</code>, a group with no enabled operation.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>toolId</code> is the tool identity. <code>validate(program, machine)</code> already
      reports <code>MissingTool</code> by id. The pocket number is emission metadata via
      <code>PostOptions.toolNumbers</code>. Program numbers start at
      <code>post.programNumberBase</code> (default 1000) plus the index, and the fanuc
      <code>O</code> number reads <code>metadata.programNumber</code>.
      <code>fromPreviousStock</code> feeds the previous tool’s diameter into a rest
      <code>3d_surface</code> pass. Spec 04 §12’s limit still applies: the previous operation is
      assumed to have cut the same Z levels with the same allowance.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retract before a tool change is opt-in. Turning it on for existing single-tool goldens
      would move those files. Leave the default off unless the setup asked for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="api" class="doc-section">
    <h2>API</h2>
    <p>
      Spec 14 §4 adds a group <code>camSetup</code>. The legacy routes in spec 05 stay as they
      are. <code>generate</code> carries resolved elements because core does not load drawings.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Route</th><th>Body</th><th>Result</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>POST /api/cam/setups/plan</code></td><td><code>&#123; setup &#125;</code></td><td><code>SetupPlan</code>. Dry run. No geometry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /api/cam/setups/generate</code></td><td><code>&#123; setup &#125;</code></td><td><code>GeneratedSetup</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>POST /api/cam/setups/validate</code></td><td><code>&#123; setup &#125;</code></td><td><code>&#123; issues &#125;</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Those three routes are specified. The group file and the sidecar handler file are not in
      the repository. Do not call them against a running sidecar until both exist and an HTTP
      test covers them. Saved multi-operation documents are a related, separate path: spec 05’s
      change log says a portable <code>CamProgramDocument</code> may carry an optional ordered
      <code>operations</code> array inside <code>Toolpath.data</code>. That persistence reuses
      the toolpath tables. It is not the setup planner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="frames" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Work frames</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/core/src/cam/setup.ts</code> is already exported from the CAM barrel. It
      defines a rigid right-handed <code>WorkFrame</code>, a revisioned
      <code>ManufacturingSetup</code> (stock and fixture envelopes in world coordinates, tool
      assemblies), and <code>validateSetup</code> for finite geometry, an orthonormal frame,
      unique tool and fixture ids, and clearance-envelope errors. Spec 04’s change log calls this
      setup validation, not collision certification. Use it when you are placing a work coordinate
      system. Use spec 14 when you are ordering operations into programs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The planner must not read the database or the clock. Geometry is an argument.</li>
      <li>Callers must not concatenate finished G-code strings to simulate a tool change.</li>
      <li>The setup HTTP group must not replace <code>POST /api/toolpaths/generate</code>. Spec 14 says the legacy routes stay.</li>
      <li>Enabling <code>retractBeforeToolChange</code> by default would change goldens. The post leaves it off unless the setup asks.</li>
      <li>Desktop persistence of setups is pending. Do not invent a second toolpath table for it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
